I take this magnesium glycinate about 30 minutes before bed, right alongside the 1mg fast-dissolve melatonin I already reviewed. But it's worth saying plainly: this one earns its spot on its own merits, not just as a sidekick to the melatonin.

Magnesium glycinate has a real reputation for being one of the more relaxing forms of magnesium you can take, and the reason comes down to what it's bound to. It's chelated with glycine, an amino acid that also functions as an inhibitory neurotransmitter — meaning it helps the nervous system dial down rather than rev up. Glycine can cross the blood-brain barrier and, once it does, is thought to stimulate calming receptor activity in the brain. So you're not just getting magnesium here; you're getting a delivery mechanism that's doing some of the calming work on its own.

The other reason I stick with the glycinate form specifically: no gastric issues. Some other common forms of magnesium, such as citrate and oxide, especially pull water into the gut at effective doses and can cause loose stool, which is a genuinely common complaint. Glycine chelation protects the mineral from osmotic effects and makes it noticeably gentler on the stomach. That's not just marketing language; it's a well-documented reason glycinate gets recommended over cheaper mineral salts in the first place. For me, and for a lot of other people who've tried both, it's a real, noticeable difference.

It's also a highly absorbable form. Rather than relying on the pathways your body uses for standalone minerals, the glycine binding lets it hitch a ride through a dipeptide transport pathway that's considerably more efficient. In plain terms, more of what's in the capsule actually gets used rather than passing through.

Here's the part that surprised me a little: this brand is genuinely cost-effective compared to other major supplement brands that sell the same form and dose, and, as far as I can tell, the quality holds up. It's third-party tested by an accredited lab, with a published Certificate of Analysis covering potency and contamination screening — which matters in a supplement category where no one is required to prove the bottle contains what the label says. I haven't found a corner being cut to hit that lower price point.
